  "text": "Modern quantum field theory is reaching a stage where it must adopt the precise language of contemporary pure mathematics to overcome its notorious infinite results. These infinities cannot be handled properly unless the notion of convergence is defined rigorously. In ordinary analysis of real and complex numbers, one can often get correct answers by formally manipulating power series, but those numbers form topological algebras, and only after the introduction of limit‑based definitions did mathematicians learn to separate meaningful statements from nonsense. The same issue appears when dealing with algebras of infinitely many degrees of freedom: algebraic tricks give results, yet they usually need a topological foundation. A common way to supply such a foundation is to view the observable quantities as operators acting on a space of square‑integrable functions. This approach, rarely used in the standard electromagnetic theory, yields a fully rigorous framework that still follows physical intuition. The exposition is split into two complementary parts—one focusing on the mathematical structure, the other on the physical interpretation—so readers can adjust the balance to their taste. Simplifications are possible by ignoring technical domain issues, certain group‑theoretic details, and advanced covering‑group constructions, which are not required for deriving the classical field equations."
